				\begin{document}$$\approx 300 \mu m \mathrm{to} 5 mm$$\end{document}) reflects both the fine structural thicknesses and the overall size of individual components within the middle ear. Surgeons need to perform their procedures with precise accuracy because the ossicular chain has fragile structures. The effective removal of diseased tissue using laser-based techniques shows good potential, as these methods enable controlled tissue removal with minimal damage to the surrounding healthy tissue and the delicate middle ear ossicles. Preservation of the ossicular chain has been shown to be associated with improved postoperative hearing outcomes, and laser-assisted techniques may facilitate this by enabling precise tissue removal21–23.

