				\begin{document}$${{\mu }^\prime}_{s}\approx 4-7 {mm}^{-1}$$\end{document}, subcutaneous adipose tissue: µ's~1.4mm-1)37,54. To simulate clinically realistic conditions, the samples were covered with a thin layer of blood, and single pulses, partly in overlapping sequences, were applied to replicate surgical resection paths. The pulse duration and power range were selected based on previous pulsed-laser studies and the concept of thermal relaxation time, aiming to limit heat diffusion and confine the effect to superficial tissue layers24,38,39. The main objective is to determine parameter values which support both safe operation and reliable application results. The analysis of the influence of laser power, fiber distance, angle of incidence, and pulse overlap should show how these parameters determine the ablation behavior of the tissue. In addition, samples of human cholesteatoma and middle ear ossicles were used to test the transferability of the results to clinical conditions. In the long term, the findings may contribute to improving the precision of laser-assisted procedures, potentially reducing the risk of thermal or mechanical damage to the ossicles, and thus may supporting the preservation of hearing.

